			<div class="content" id="post-content-4848220">
									

<p>It looks like you're trying to set up a mystery in your central narrative (how did this man get all these quarters?) but the answer to the mystery isn't particularly interesting (an anomaly that makes quarters out of nothing).</p>
<p>Compare your mystery to something like <a href="/scp-5005">SCP-5005</a> (spoilers ahead BTW). One of the mysteries 5005 sets up is &quot;where did this giant magic lamp come from?&quot; and the answer is &quot;it's the lure of a massive anglerfish&quot;. Once you know the answer, the complete situation &quot;this town exists in a sea of nothingness because of the light of a giant dead anglerfish's lure&quot; is a lot more interesting than &quot;this guy has a lot of quarters because he has an SCP that makes quarters&quot;.</p>
<p>The simplest solution would be changing your anomaly so that there's some deeper implication to making all those quarters. Say, for example, that the anomaly is a machine that grinds cute baby animals into a pulp and then turns them into quarters. Now, your complete situation isn't just &quot;this guy has a lot of quarters because he has an SCP that makes quarters&quot;&#8212; it's &quot;this guy has a lot of quarters because he <em>was willing to grind up tons of cute baby animals for money</em>.&quot;</p>

							</div>

								
					
